@fresh-database-user-creation-and-domain-counts
Feature: Fresh Database User Creation and Domain Counts
  As an operator standing up a new deployment of the user service
  I want POST /users and GET /users/count-by-domain to answer correctly on a freshly created database
  So that a new deployment serves callers without anyone adding a column by hand

  Background:
    Given the user service is running

  # Why: The first half of the ask — creating a user must work before any other data exists.
  @key-example @smoke
  Scenario: Creating a user on a freshly created database answers 201
    Given a freshly created database with no users
    When a POST request to /users is sent with the email "ana@example.com"
    Then the status code should be 201
    And the response body should carry the new user's id and the email "ana@example.com"

  # Why: The second half of the ask — the count route must answer before any user exists.
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=low] GET /users/count-by-domain answers 200 with an empty list when no users exist, rather than 404
  @key-example @smoke
  Scenario: Counting users by domain on a freshly created database answers 200
    Given a freshly created database with no users
    When a GET request to /users/count-by-domain is sent
    Then the status code should be 200
    And the response body should be an empty list of domain counts

  # Why: The two routes together — users created through the service are counted by the service.
  @key-example
  Scenario: Counts by domain reflect the users created since the database was made
    Given a freshly created database
    And POST requests to /users have created two users at "example.com" and one at "test.org"
    When a GET request to /users/count-by-domain is sent
    Then the status code should be 200
    And the response body should list "example.com" with a count of 2 and "test.org" with a count of 1

  # Why: Just-inside boundary — one user is the smallest non-empty count.
  @boundary
  Scenario: A single user yields a single domain count
    Given a freshly created database
    And a POST request to /users has created one user at "solo.example"
    When a GET request to /users/count-by-domain is sent
    Then the status code should be 200
    And the response body should list exactly one domain, "solo.example", with a count of 1

  # Why: Just-inside boundary — the first user written to a new database can be read back by id.
  @boundary
  Scenario: The first user created is readable by id
    Given a freshly created database with no users
    And a POST request to /users has answered 201 with a new user id
    When a GET request to /users/{id} is sent for that id
    Then the status code should be 200
    And the response body should carry the email that was posted

  # Why: A bad request on a fresh database must be refused for the right reason, not because of a missing column.
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=low] A POST /users body with no email is rejected with 400
  @negative
  Scenario: Creating a user without an email is rejected on a freshly created database
    Given a freshly created database with no users
    When a POST request to /users is sent with no email in the body
    Then the status code should be 400
    And the response body should say the email is missing

  # Why: The uniqueness rule must hold from the first user onwards.
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=low] A second user with an email already taken is rejected with 409
  @negative
  Scenario: Creating a user with an email already taken is rejected
    Given a freshly created database
    And a POST request to /users has created a user with the email "dup@example.com"
    When a second POST request to /users is sent with the email "dup@example.com"
    Then the status code should be 409
    And the response body should say the email is already in use

  # Why: The failure that stopped the run was on first writes — two at once is the sharpest form of it.
  @edge-case
  Scenario: Two users created at the same moment on a freshly created database both answer 201
    Given a freshly created database with no users
    When two POST requests to /users are sent at the same moment with different emails
    Then the status code of each should be 201
    And a GET request to /users/count-by-domain should then answer 200 with both users counted
